    "knowledge_point": "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cosecant Function",
    "problem": "Find ${f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ if $f(x)=ln⁡x-5csc⁡x.$",
    "worked_solution": "We take the derivative of each term and then add them, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x-5csc⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}-5(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}+5csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ in the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}+5csc⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})cot⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }+5\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
